   "id": "movigo",
   "title": "Movigo logistics platform",
   "url": "/projects/movigo",
   "text": "Movigo logistics platform (Production platforms; In production; Apr 2026 - present). Live delivery marketplace connecting retailers and customers with 2-, 3- and 4-wheeler drivers: a Node.js dispatch and payments backend, Flutter driver and retailer apps on Google Play and the App Store, and the operations console.\nStack: Node.js, Express, MongoDB, Flutter, Pusher, Razorpay, Firebase Cloud Messaging, OSRM.\nLinks: Movigo Driver on Google Play https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.app.movigodriver; Movigo Retailer on Google Play https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.app.movigocustomer; Movigo Retailer on the App Store https://apps.apple.com/in/app/movigo-retailer-app/id6793887251.\n8 Flutter test files across the driver and retailer apps.\nMovigo: dispatch, payments and two apps for a live delivery business\nMovigo connects retailers and customers in Indore with drivers on two-, three- and four-wheelers. I am the company’s only engineer: I rebuilt the product after an outsourced team delivered designs but no working software, and I run the backend, both apps and the infrastructure today.\nContext: Retailers book a delivery, nearby drivers get a ringing offer, one driver wins it, and the booking is tracked live until a PIN confirms delivery. The operations team needs to change prices, zones and commission without waiting for an app release, and drivers work on low-end Android phones that kill background apps.\nRole: Founding Engineer & Tech Lead since June 2026. I joined in April 2026 as App Developer and was promoted to Lead App Developer in May. I own the backend, the Flutter driver and retailer apps, the admin console, store releases and production support.\nStatus: In production. Driver and retailer apps on Google Play (1K+ installs each in October 2026); retailer app on the App Store.\nSystem map: Clients: Driver app (Flutter, Android), Retailer app (Flutter, Android and iOS), Admin console (Operations staff), Owner panel (Next.js, business accounts); Node.js API: Six route groups (app, admin, panel, analytics, field officer, public), Dispatch loop (every 15 seconds), Scheduled jobs (timeouts, wallets, referrals, reports); Services: MongoDB (geo-indexed driver locations), Pusher (live tracking and chat), Razorpay (wallets and fees), Firebase Cloud Messaging, OSRM (road distances)\nDecision: One conditional write decides who gets the job. When several drivers accept the same booking, the accept handler updates the booking only if it is still pending. MongoDB applies that update atomically, so exactly one driver wins and everyone else is told the job is gone. Eligibility checks (KYC, wallet balance, vehicle type, no other active job) run before the write.\nDecision: Pusher instead of Socket.IO. Live tracking and chat moved from Socket.IO to Pusher channels per driver, booking and chat, so real-time updates no longer depend on long-lived connections to the API server. The retailer app kept its old socket calls as no-ops during the move, so screens already in users’ hands did not have to change.\nDecision: Business rules as data. Pricing slabs, commission, surge multipliers, zones and the dispatch radius live in a settings document behind a short cache that is cleared whenever an admin saves. Operations can change a price and see it on the next request, without an app release, and pricing reads fall from more than 100 a second to none within 60 seconds.\nDecision: Road distance instead of straight lines. Dispatch first widened a straight-line search radius as a booking aged. In July 2026 it moved to a road-distance cutoff from OSRM, fetched in one distance-matrix call per booking instead of one call per driver, so drivers are not offered jobs that are close on a map but far by road.\nPayments without double charges: Wallet top-ups and fees go through Razorpay. Every payment confirmation is verified before anything is credited, and each payment is credited exactly once, even when a callback is retried. Drivers whose wallet falls below the configured minimum stop receiving bookings until they top up, with a gentler threshold for new two-wheeler drivers on trial rides.\nKeeping drivers reachable: Booking offers have to ring on cheap Android phones that aggressively kill background work. The driver app combines a location stream with a heartbeat, pauses duplicate location posts, and has an in-app guide for the battery settings of specific phone makers, with its own test.\nReleases: The driver and retailer apps ship to Google Play and the retailer app to the App Store, with iOS builds automated on Codemagic.\nFact: API routes: 400+ across six route groups (source: Route declarations in the backend source, October 2026)\nFact: Data models: 40 Mongoose models (source: Backend models folder, October 2026)\nFact: Scheduled jobs: 8, plus a 15-second dispatch loop (source: Backend cron folder)\nFact: Store presence: 2 apps on Google Play, 1 on the App Store (source: Public store listings, October 2026)\nFact: Google Play installs: 1K+ per app (source: Public store listings, October 2026)\nQuality: The driver and retailer apps have tests for the fare calculator, the booking card, image upload and the battery-settings guide.\nQuality: Bookings, payments and wallet changes rely on atomic writes and idempotency flags, and admin actions are recorded in an audit log."
