   "id": "toolforge",
   "title": "ToolForge",
   "url": "/projects/toolforge",
   "text": "ToolForge (Agentic AI and LLM applications; Working; Sep 2026). Point it at an OpenAPI spec and agents design an MCP server, test it against the live API and audit it for security problems, with a person approving the tool plan.\nStack: Python, LangGraph, MCP, Pydantic, Typer, pytest.\n24 pytest tests.\nToolForge: agents that build, test and audit MCP servers\nThousands of APIs have no Model Context Protocol server. ToolForge reads an OpenAPI spec, has an agent design the tools an assistant actually needs, generates the server, tests it against the live API and audits it for security problems.\nContext: For developers who want an agent to use an existing REST API. It runs as a command-line tool, as a web interface, or as an auditor for any MCP server, including ones it did not build.\nRole: Designed and built alone.\nStatus: Working, as a command-line tool and a web interface. Three example builds are checked in with their scorecards.\nBuild pipeline: Read spec (deterministic parse; the endpoint list is ground truth) -> Design tools (agent picks endpoints and writes names) -> Check plan (unknown endpoints or missing params go back) -> Human review (approve, reject or revise) -> Generate server (spec strings escaped) -> Validate (load in a separate process) -> Live test (tester agent calls the real API) -> Security audit and scorecard\nDecision: Pass or fail comes from recorded calls. The tester agent chains real calls (list, take an id, fetch it). Whether a tool works is decided from the recorded responses, never from the model’s opinion. Failures caused by bad design send the plan back to the designer, two rounds by default.\nDecision: A person approves the plan. The graph pauses with LangGraph interrupt() until someone approves, rejects or revises the tool plan. Unattended mode exists but needs an explicit flag that also allows write calls, so it is meant for sandbox APIs only.\nDecision: A hostile spec cannot inject code. Every string from the spec goes into the generated server through repr(), and the audit flags hardcoded secrets, invisible characters, prompt injection in descriptions, destructive tools, plain-HTTP base URLs and unauthenticated writes.\nFact: PokeAPI: 8 tools, 8 of 8 live calls passed, security 100/100 (source: examples/pokeapi/scorecard.json)\nFact: Swagger Petstore: The tester sent design bugs back to the designer, which fixed them on the second round; security 84/100 (source: examples/petstore/scorecard.json)\nFact: xkcd: 2 tools, 2 of 2 passed, security 90/100 (plain-HTTP URL flagged) (source: examples/xkcd/scorecard.json)\nQuality: 24 pytest tests covering the spec loader (including reference cycles and Swagger 2 rejection), the pipeline and live stages.\nQuality: Every build writes a scorecard, the full call log and per-step timings.\nQuality: A 102-endpoint spec fits a 16,000-character prompt budget, so builds run on free-tier models."
